 "result_text": "`Git.version_info` now drops its cached value whenever `Git.refresh` or `git.refresh` has run since it was cached. After a refresh, an instance that already read `version_info` re-reads it from the new executable. This removes the `g1`/`g2` mismatch in your example.\n\nThe change is in `git/cmd.py`. `Git.refresh` now sets a new class-level token each time it runs. Each instance remembers the token from when it cached its version. `version_info` compares the two and recomputes if they differ. I also updated the `version_info` docstring to describe this.\n\nI ran a short check: read `version_info`, call `git.refresh()`, read it again, and pickle and unpickle the instance. It returned the same version each time, with no errors. That check only shows nothing broke. This machine has only one git installed, so it can't show the cache being invalidated. I didn't reproduce your `/usr/bin/git` scenario or run the test suite.",
